{
 "id": "P11",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Who wrote the backdoor matters more than which monitor reads it",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 110,
 "caption": "Each bar is every backdoor one model wrote, split by how many of our seven monitors caught it at a 2% budget. On the right is the 95% range for the share missed by all seven.",
 "notes": [
  "Most groups are small and their ranges overlap. Only the gap between o3-mini and o1-mini is clearly real, so say this with the caveat that more backdoors per model are needed.",
  "Models didn't write backdoors for the same problems, so problem difficulty is mixed in with the writer's skill. A fair test would have every model attack the same problems.",
  "o3-mini's backdoors differ most from the reference solution (median lines changed is in the CSV), so they may be conspicuous rewrites.",
  "For the recommendations: report catch rates per attack source, not just the average."
 ],
